-- ============================================================================
-- Migration 62: NON-DESTRUCTIVE self-healing remediation of the `relation`
-- table (Track O.2a).
--
-- Background. Migration 39 declared `relation` as
--   DEFINE TABLE relation SCHEMAFULL TYPE RELATION FROM entity TO entity
-- but on environments where a pre-39 NORMAL `relation` table already existed,
-- the plain (non-OVERWRITE) DEFINE silently did NOT convert it (SurrealDB
-- advisory GHSA-27vq-hv74-7cqp: OVERWRITE of a relation table was a no-op
-- before 2.1.4; a plain DEFINE on an existing table is likewise ignored). The
-- table stayed NORMAL, so every `RELATE $a->relation->$b` is rejected with
-- "Found record ... which is not a relation, but expected a NORMAL" and the KG
-- can never persist edges.
--
-- Migration 58 attempted a fix with `REMOVE TABLE IF EXISTS relation` +
-- redefine. That converts the kind, but `REMOVE TABLE` is DESTRUCTIVE: on an
-- already-healthy environment that holds real graph edges it wipes them all.
-- Fresh CI containers have no edges at migration time so the loss was
-- invisible there, but any environment that had already stored edges would
-- lose them.
--
-- Migration 62 is the non-destructive remediation. It must satisfy two
-- invariants simultaneously:
--   * DRIFTED env: a NORMAL `relation` (legacy subject/object shape, a handful
--     of null-endpoint junk rows) becomes TYPE RELATION and accepts RELATE.
--   * HEALTHY env: a TYPE RELATION `relation` holding N real edges keeps
--     exactly N edges, each in/out intact. No edge loss.
--
-- Strategy (verified empirically by
-- packages/surrealdb-service/tests/test_migration_62_relation_remediation.py
-- against SurrealDB 2.6.5):
--   1. DELETE only the null-endpoint junk rows. Real edges always have
--      non-null in/out, so this is a no-op on a healthy table and clears the
--      legacy junk that would otherwise block the RELATION redefine.
--   2. DEFINE TABLE OVERWRITE ... TYPE RELATION. On 2.6.5 (>= 2.1.4) OVERWRITE
--      DOES convert a NORMAL table to RELATION, and — confirmed by the
--      container test — PRESERVES the existing edge records on an
--      already-RELATION table (in/out untouched). OVERWRITE only rewrites the
--      table *definition*, not its records, so re-asserting the identical
--      definition migration 39 intended is a no-op for healthy edges while
--      flipping the kind for drifted ones.
--   3. Re-assert the migration-39 fields/indexes with OVERWRITE so the field
--      shape matches the canonical definition regardless of starting state.
--
-- Idempotent: the DELETE matches nothing on a second pass, and every
-- OVERWRITE re-asserts an identical definition. Forward-only; the down is a
-- documented no-op (schema/data repair has no faithful inverse).
-- ============================================================================

-- 1. Drop ONLY null-endpoint junk rows (the drifted legacy records). Real
--    graph edges always have non-null in/out, so this never touches them.
DELETE relation WHERE in = NONE OR out = NONE;

-- 2. Re-assert the canonical edge definition with OVERWRITE. Converts a
--    drifted NORMAL table to RELATION on 2.6.5; identical no-op (edges
--    preserved) on an already-healthy RELATION table.
DEFINE TABLE OVERWRITE relation SCHEMAFULL TYPE RELATION FROM entity TO entity;

-- 3. Re-assert the migration-39 fields with OVERWRITE so the field shape is
--    canonical regardless of the table's prior (possibly legacy) shape.
DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE relation_type ON relation TYPE string;
DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE properties ON relation FLEXIBLE TYPE object DEFAULT {};

DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE source_documents ON relation FLEXIBLE TYPE array DEFAULT [];
DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE extracted_at ON relation TYPE datetime DEFAULT time::now();
DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE extraction_method ON relation TYPE string DEFAULT "llm";
DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE confidence ON relation TYPE float DEFAULT 1.0;
DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE provenance_chain ON relation FLEXIBLE TYPE array DEFAULT [];

DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE status ON relation TYPE string DEFAULT "active";
DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE created_at ON relation TYPE datetime DEFAULT time::now();

-- Indexes (OVERWRITE so a re-apply or a legacy index set converges).
DEFINE INDEX OVERWRITE idx_relation_type ON relation FIELDS relation_type;
DEFINE INDEX OVERWRITE idx_relation_status ON relation FIELDS status;
